      "body": "Closes #1612\n\nThe `headers` rule applies its headers to `event.res.errHeaders` after the handler has run, and `errHeaders` takes precedence over `HTTPError.headers` (in h3's `errorResponse` and in Nitro's `createErrorHeaders`). So a handler could not change a rule header on its own error response. In Nitro (since nitrojs/nitro#4891), a 404 for a missing asset under `/build/**` got `cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`, which lets a CDN or browser cache the 404.\n\n### Change\n\nAn `HTTPError`, thrown or returned, that carries one of the rule's keys in its own `headers` keeps that value. The rule skips the `errHeaders` write for that key only:\n\n```ts\nthrow new HTTPError({ status: 404, headers: { \"cache-control\": \"no-store\" } });\n// → cache-control: no-store; the rule's other headers still apply\n```\n\n### Why not detect changes to `errHeaders` (the issue's suggestion)\n\nAn earlier revision of this PR did that: it skipped keys whose `errHeaders` value changed during `next()`. A review showed that a change there isn't a reliable signal:\n\n- **CORS helpers:** `appendCorsHeaders`/`handleCors` in user middleware (Nitro `server/middleware/`) write `errHeaders` inside the rule, so their values beat the rule on 4xx/5xx while the rule still won on 2xx. On main the rule wins both.\n- **Appends:** `errHeaders.append(\"vary\", …)` dropped the rule's `vary` on errors.\n- **Nested rules:** a stacked or mounted `headers` rule needed extra tracking.\n- **Opt-out mechanics:** deleting a key couldn't opt out, and returning (instead of throwing) under an inner `cache` rule didn't work.\n- **Cost:** it added per-request work on the success path.\n\n`HTTPError.headers` is an explicit statement about that error response, and the check costs nothing on successful requests.\n\n### Unchanged (pinned by tests that pass on main and on this branch)\n\n- 2xx responses always get the rule headers (h3js/h3-rules#5 ordering).\n- `errHeaders` staged anywhere (outer middleware, the `cors` rule, CORS helpers or appends inside the rule) is still overridden by the rule on errors.\n- An error `Response` the handler builds itself (e.g. an upstream 404 through `proxy`) still gets the rule header. Only `HTTPError` opts out.\n- Stacked `headers` rules: the outer one wins on 2xx and on errors.\n\n### Notes\n\n- **`return` under a `cache` rule:** on a route that also has a `cache` rule, *return* the `HTTPError` and the `cache` rule renders it to a plain `Response` first, so the opt-out is lost. *Throwing* works.\n- **Tree-shaking:** `HTTPError.isError` is inlined so that `headers`-only bundles don't pull in `error.ts` (`treeshake.test.ts` budget).\n- **Nitro follow-up:** in `static.ts`, replace `event.res.headers.delete(\"Cache-Control\")` + `throw new HTTPError({ status: 404 })` with `throw new HTTPError({ status: 404, headers: { \"cache-control\": \"no-store\" } })`.\n\n🤖 Generated with AI assistant\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n<!-- coderabbit-ai-generated:v1 -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **Bug Fixes**\n  * Configured route-rule headers are now applied consistently to successful and error responses, including errors that are thrown or returned.\n  * Configured headers override staged error headers and headers on returned error responses. For qualifying HTTP errors, an error’s own header value is preserved in the error response.\n  * Configured headers continue to override matching headers set by a handler on a successful response.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
